Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'Mabel Fern and the Missing a stopped watch', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Seaside Mysteries', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Tea and Tidings', 'text': "librarian Miss Faye arrived before the tea was poured, full of the news: the charming stranger had been seen about Willowmere village at dawn, asking questions nobody liked. 'Mabel Fern,' said librarian Miss Faye, 'there's a mystery brewing, and you're the only one with the nose for it.'\n\nBefore bed she wrote in her journal: Day one. The game is on. Tomorrow I walk the hedgerows and ask the questions no one else will ask. courage in quiet lives — that is what this is about, though no one says so yet.\n\nBy noon the whole of Willowmere village was whispering. Mabel Fern listened at the bakery, at the post office, at the garden gate, collecting every whisper like crumbs. Each crumb led back to a stopped watch, and a stopped watch led somewhere darker."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Missing Piece', 'text': "That night she dreamed of Willowmere village with all its windows lit, and every window hiding something. She woke knowing the next step: confront the one person who had not been questioned — the charming stranger himself.\n\nA note came then, unsigned: 'Stop asking about a stopped watch.' Mabel Fern read it twice, smiled, and pinned it to her board. Threats, she told librarian Miss Faye, are confessions in a hurry.\n\nMabel Fern began with Willowmere village itself, walking every lane with a stopped watch wrapped in a handkerchief. The hedgerows of Willowmere village had seen everything, she always said; one had only to ask the right people in the right order."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'Questions at Dusk', 'text': "Mabel Fern found the charming stranger at the edge of Willowmere village, packing a bag in a great hurry. 'Leaving us?' she asked pleasantly, and the charming stranger's smile did not reach the eyes. Every answer was smooth, and every smooth answer was a lie.\n\nBut Mabel Fern shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a stopped watch was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone.\n\nBack in the parlor, Mabel Fern laid out the evidence: the landlord's tale, the parish gap, the threatening note, the too-perfect description. librarian Miss Faye whistled. 'It's a noose,' they said, 'and the charming stranger tied it.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "the charming stranger appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Mabel Fern could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Willowmere village. 'I never meant —' began the charming stranger, and could not finish.\n\nThey went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a stopped watch. The whole sad story lay in the mud.\n\nThey walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. librarian Miss Faye said nothing, but their silence was agreement. courage in quiet lives — even on the worst night, Mabel Fern thought, especially then."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "a stopped watch was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Mabel Fern declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nthe charming stranger's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Willowmere village was stern, but Willowmere village was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish.\n\nThat evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Mabel Fern wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. courage in quiet lives — I shall drink tea to that."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "the charming stranger, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Mabel Fern read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nA week later Willowmere village was itself again: the fete committee argued about bunting, the bakery smelled of scones, and a stopped watch sat in its proper place, no longer a mystery but a keepsake with a story.\n\nlibrarian Miss Faye brought flowers — 'for the best detective in the county,' they said, gruff as ever. Mabel Fern put them in water and told them not to be silly, but she kept the card."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

