 Agnes Thimble laid out the evidence: the landlord's tale, the parish gap, the threatening note, the too-perfect description. librarian Miss Faye whistled. 'It's a noose,' they said, 'and the masked visitor tied it.'\n\nAt dusk she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a silver locket in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "They walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. librarian Miss Faye said nothing, but their silence was agreement. truth will out — even on the worst night, Agnes Thimble thought, especially then.\n\nThe storm that had threatened Hollyhock Lane all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Hollyhock Lane. In the midst of it came a hammering at Agnes Thimble's door: librarian Miss Faye, soaked, with news — the masked visitor had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nThey went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a silver locket. The whole sad story lay in the mud."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the masked visitor's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Hollyhock Lane was stern, but Hollyhock Lane was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish.\n\nThe constable did his duty, but Agnes Thimble had already done hers: she had made the truth plain, so that no whisper could twist it. librarian Miss Faye squeezed her hand. 'Neatest case I ever saw,' they whispered.\n\nThat evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Agnes Thimble wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. truth will out — I shall drink tea to that."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'the masked visitor, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Agnes Thimble read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nA week later Hollyhock Lane was itself again: the fete committee argued about bunting, the bakery smelled of scones, and a silver locket sat in its proper place, no longer a mystery but a keepsake with a story.\n\nOn Sunday Agnes Thimble walked the hedgerows with no case at all, just the sun and the birds and the good green smell of Hollyhock Lane. A detective, she reflected, is only a neighbor who pays attention.'}]}

